US Visa Overview — Every Type You Will Encounter on a Call
All US visas split into two categories: immigrant (permanent) and non-immigrant (temporary). The category determines whether work authorisation is permanent or restricted.
Immigrant Visa
Green Card Path — Permanent
•	Intent: permanent residence and eventual US citizenship
•	Work auth: unlimited — any employer, any role
•	Duration: permanent (Green Card renewed every 10 years)
•	Result: Lawful Permanent Resident (LPR)
•	Path to citizenship: 3 years (married to USC) or 5 years (other paths)
Non-Immigrant Visa
Temporary Stay — Restricted
•	Intent: temporary presence for specific purpose (work, study, business)
•	Work auth: restricted — tied to employer, purpose, or duration
•	Duration: varies by type (3 months to 6 years typically)
•	Result: worker must leave, extend, or change status when visa expires
•	Examples: F-1 (student), H-1B (worker), L-1 (intra-company), TN (NAFTA)
⭐
The Recruiter's Work Auth Hierarchy
Green Card = EAD > USC > TN > H-1B

Green Card and USC are always the cleanest submissions — no expiry tracking, no employer dependency, no transfer delays. When you find a Green Card candidate, submit fast — every other agency is looking for them too. EAD is good if the expiry date covers the contract period. H-1B is the most complex — employer-tied, transfer required, cap lottery risk.
