H-1B Specialty Occupation Visa
H-1B is the most common work visa for skilled professionals. It is also the most restrictive and the most often client-rejected visa type you will encounter.
H-1B Visa
Specialty Occupation — Employer-Tied
H1B
PurposeTemporary employment for "specialty occupation" workers — roles requiring at minimum a bachelor's degree. Common in IT, engineering, accounting, finance, medicine.
DurationInitial 3 years. Extension to 6 years maximum. Further extensions if green card process is in progress.
Annual Cap85,000 visas/year (65,000 regular + 20,000 US master's holders). If demand exceeds cap → lottery. Recent odds: ~1 in 3.
Employer-TiedTied to the SPECIFIC EMPLOYER who sponsored it. Candidate cannot simply change jobs — must get H-1B portability transfer via AC21.
Prevailing WageEmployer MUST pay the H-1B worker the prevailing wage for the role and location (set by DOL). Cannot underpay to save costs.
Cost to SponsorUSCIS filing ($460) + legal fees ($1,500–$3,000) + optional premium processing ($2,500) = $4,000–$7,000+ total
Most staffing agencies do NOT sponsor new H-1B visas — the cost, lottery risk, and timeline make it impractical for contract placements. If a candidate says "I need H-1B sponsorship" → your agency almost certainly cannot help. Be empathetic but clear: "We don't sponsor H-1B visas — you'd need to find a direct employer. If you obtain your own H-1B later, we'd love to work with you."
